+/-! `FlatMap<I, U, F>` and `Flatten<I>` (core flatten.rs, rustc 1.95) delegate every method to
+    `FlattenCompat { iter: Fuse<..>, frontiter: Option<U>, backiter: Option<U> }`. The model keeps
+    the outer iterator with its `Fuse` flag (`outerDone`), the front inner iterator (`inner`,
+    active while `frontiter` is `Some`) and the back inner iterator (`back`, active while
+    `backiter` is `Some`). An inactive slot retains the last inner state but is never polled.
+
+    The `Iterator` methods pull from the front and the `DoubleEndedIterator` methods from the
+    back with the same schedule, the roles of the two inner slots exchanged: the near inner
+    first, then the outer (each converted outer item becomes the near inner), then the far inner
+    (`next` on the back inner once the outer is exhausted, `next_back` on the front inner; the
+    folds fold the front inner, the outer, the back inner, and the back folds the other way
+    round). The model states this once over one end (`End`: `next`/`fold`/`try_fold`/`advance_by`
+    from the front, `next_back`/`rfold`/`try_rfold`/`advance_back_by` from the back); the back
+    methods run it on the state whose inner slots are exchanged (`swap`).
+
+    `OneShot` inners (the `one_shot` flag of their `Iterator` dictionary: option and result
+    iterators, `Once`, arrays of length at most one, and the adapters of these) select the
+    specialization of both traits, which ignores `frontiter` and `backiter` and pulls each
+    converted outer item once with `next` (also from the back). It never stores an inner, so the
+    model's slots stay inactive for these inners; the shared code which drains them is then the
+    identity, and the far inner is never pulled. -/
